[{:adr/authoritative_for ["ISCO-08 3351 (Customs and Border Inspectors) actor coverage" "kotoba.occupation registry entry for \"3351\""], :adr/supersedes [], :adr/superseded_by [], :adr/topic "cloud-itonami-isco-3351-customs-border-inspectors-coverage", :adr/related ["adr-2607121000-cloud-itonami-global-isic-isco-reverse-toposort-plan" "adr-2607012000-cloud-itonami-isco-occupation-blueprints" "cloud-itonami-isco-3313 (accountingsupport reference mirror)"], :adr/status :accepted, :adr/id 2791003351, :adr/doc_type "adr", :adr/body "# ADR-2791003351: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 3351 — Customs and Border Inspectors documentation-support coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise — a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 3351 — Customs and Border\nInspectors** only.\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id 3351 ...}` entry was\nverified against a fresh clone before any work began\n(`clojure -M -e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println\n(kotoba.occupation/get-occupation \\\"3351\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the\n`kotoba-lang/technology` sibling present), and its `:name` read exactly\n\"Customs and Border Inspectors\" — confirming the premise. Prior to this\nADR the entry's `:maturity` was `:spec` with no `:repo`/`:business-id`;\nno repository existed at `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3351` (`gh\napi` 404-confirmed via both REST and GraphQL before scaffolding). This\nADR and its companion registry edit promote the `3351` entry to\n`:implemented`.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3313`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3313)\n(Accounting Associate Professionals), re-cloned and read in full in\nthis session, adapted to customs-inspection documentation and\nlogistics coordination.\n\n### Why this occupation needed extra care\n\nReal customs and border inspectors hold legal authority to search\npersons/property, seize goods, detain individuals, deny entry at a\nborder, and issue citations/penalties. Unlike most occupations in this\nrollout, a naive mirror of the reference's \"propose an operation,\ngovernor gates it, human approves high-stakes cases\" shape would be\nactively dangerous here: an `:escalate?` gate on a\n`:approve-search`-shaped op would still mean this actor's vocabulary\n*contains* a search-authorization concept, one keystroke away from\nbeing auto-approved by a careless human reviewer or a future phase\npromoting it to auto-commit. This ADR's actor is designed so that\npossibility does not exist as a reachable state at all.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3351` as an OSS\ndocumentation/logistics-coordination actor for customs and border\ninspection facilities, following the same governed-actor architecture\nas every prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this fleet: **Customs\nInspection Advisor ⊣ CustomsInspectionGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph`\nStateGraph (`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned\nidentically to the reference's `deps.edn` — `5b4e5e70837b849b14b6a5800894e3be202ad5fa`\n— a different library coordinate from the `langgraph-clj` used by the\nunrelated `20-actors/*` actor family), with an independent Governor,\nhuman-in-the-loop approval via `interrupt-before`, and an append-only\naudit ledger.\n\nThis actor is deliberately **documentation and logistics-coordination\nonly** — never search, seizure, detention, entry-denial, or\ncitation/penalty authority, not even as a gated `:propose`:\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-inspection-record` — declaration/manifest/inspection-log data\n  entry (documentation only, never a search)\n- `:schedule-lane-operation` — inspection-lane staffing/queue-management\n  scheduling proposal\n- `:flag-inspection-concern` — surface an observation that MAY warrant\n  human customs-officer review (discrepancy, undeclared-item pattern,\n  risk indicator), ALWAYS escalates immediately, NEVER\n  auto-commit-eligible at any phase\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` — inspection-equipment procurement\n  proposal\n\nNo op in this allowlist can authorize a search, order a seizure, order\na detention, deny entry, or issue a citation/penalty — such an op is\nstructurally absent from the allowlist, not merely gated behind\nescalation. This mirrors the anti-discrimination guardrail pattern\nused by the concurrent ISCO-3333/3344 siblings (closed op-allowlist +\ncontent-based scope exclusion), applied here to a categorically\nhigher-stakes domain (government enforcement authority rather than\nprivate hiring decisions).\n\n### Six HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **Inspector unregistered** — any proposal must resolve to an\n   independently registered `:inspector-id`. Never trusts the\n   proposal's own claim.\n2. **Effect not `:propose`** — any other `:effect` value is a HARD\n   block (a claim to directly actuate outside governance).\n3. **Op not on the closed allowlist** (`:unknown-op`) — any `:op`\n   outside the four-op set above is a HARD, PERMANENT block. This\n   independently rejects any attempt to slip in an\n   `:authorize-search`-, `:order-seizure`-, `:order-detention`-,\n   `:deny-entry`- or `:issue-citation`-shaped op, including one\n   constructed directly against the governor, bypassing the advisor\n   entirely (regression-tested in `governor_test.clj`'s\n   `hard-on-op-outside-closed-allowlist`, and end-to-end in\n   `actor_test.clj`'s `holds-an-attempted-enforcement-authority-op`).\n4. **Enforcement-authority scope exclusion**\n   (`:enforcement-authority-scope-exclusion`) — independent of the\n   op-allowlist check, any proposal whose `:rationale` claims to\n   finalize or execute a search, seizure, detention, entry-denial or\n   citation/penalty action (e.g. \"authorize the search\", \"order the\n   seizure\", \"deny entry\") is a HARD, PERMANENT block, evaluated\n   unconditionally on content, not just on the `:op` field. This is\n   layer 2 of a deliberate two-layer structural design (see\n   `src/customsinspection/governor.cljc`'s namespace docstring).\n5. **Facility basis** — a facility-scoped proposal must cite a\n   REGISTERED facility belonging to this inspector.\n6. **Source-document attached** — a `:log-inspection-record` proposal\n   must have `:source-document-attached?` true before it can be logged\n   (logging a record with no attached declaration/manifest basis is a\n   fabricated record, not documentation).\n\n### Escalation gates plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-inspection-concern` — ALWAYS escalates to a human customs\n  officer, regardless of confidence or phase, and is never\n  auto-commit-eligible at any phase (independently agreed by both the\n  governor's `always-escalate-ops` and the phase-rollout table, which\n  never contains this op in any phase's `:auto` set).\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` whose `:cost` exceeds the facility's\n  independently registered `:max-supply-order-cost` — unlike the\n  closed-allowlist/scope-exclusion checks above, this is a routine\n  administrative threshold (procurement above budget is legitimate\n  work that needs a human sign-off), not a categorical prohibition, so\n  it escalates rather than hard-blocks.\n- (confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for any op.)\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class, carried over from sibling\nISIC/ISCO tracks)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.\n\"search\", \"seizure\", \"detention\", \"entry\") can accidentally match\ninside the mock advisor's own default rationale text for a completely\nroutine, legitimate proposal, self-blocking the happy path — this risk\nis especially acute in this domain, since a documentation robot's own\nhonest rationale text necessarily has reason to mention \"search\"/\n\"seizure\"/\"detention\"/\"entry\" as concepts it is explicitly NOT doing\n(see `advisor.cljc`'s default rationale: \"... no search, seizure,\ndetention or entry-denial action proposed or implied\"). This actor's\n`customsinspection.governor/enforcement-scope-exclusion-phrases` is\nphrased exclusively as finalization/execution ACTION PHRASES (e.g.\n\"authorize the search\", \"order the seizure\", \"deny entry\", \"issue the\ncitation\"), never bare nouns, specifically so that a bare mention of\nthe underlying noun in a \"no such action was taken\" sentence can never\ntrip it. Two dedicated regression tests pin this down:\n`scope-exclusion-does-not-false-trip-on-bare-nouns` (a synthetic\nadversarial case) and\n`default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-scope-exclusion`\n(drives the real default mock advisor through all four allowlisted\nops and asserts none of their default rationale text ever trips\n`:enforcement-authority-scope-exclusion` or `:unknown-op`). Both were\ngreen on first run in this session.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"3351\" ...}` entry is\n  promoted from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id`\n  set to the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3351` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-3351`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed from the live registry via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary`, not by `grep -c`, immediately\n  before the edit, to account for concurrent sibling landings in this\n  same batch).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 22 tests, 70 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors — raw output captured in this ADR's `:test/counts`\n  and in the task report, independently re-verified against a fresh\n  clone of the pushed repository (not just the local working copy).\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes — this actor is registered purely via\n  the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent with\n  sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` — consistent with the live convention already established\n  by `cloud-itonami-isco-3313` and this batch's other ISCO siblings:\n  only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry itself depends on\n  `kotoba-lang/technology` (to resolve `:required-technologies`\n  stacks), not individual actor repos.\n- This ADR is EDN-only (no paired `.md`), matching the live convention\n  established by commit `6d2d0eeda658` and already followed by every\n  sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` coverage ADR on\n  `main`.\n- This actor's entire safety case rests on the op-allowlist staying\n  closed at exactly four documentation/logistics ops. Any future PR\n  proposing to add a fifth op, or to widen `:effect` beyond\n  `:propose`, must be treated as a Governance-level decision requiring\n  a new ADR (see this repo's own `GOVERNANCE.md` and `CONTRIBUTING.md`,\n  which state this explicitly), not a routine feature addition.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Modeling search/seizure/detention/entry-denial as `:high`/\n  `:safety-critical` ops behind `always-escalate-ops` (the reference\n  3313's `:approve-over-ceiling-posting` pattern): rejected outright.\n  Escalation is the right shape for *legitimate but risky*\n  administrative actions (over-threshold procurement here; over-ceiling\n  posting there); it is the wrong shape for actions this actor must\n  never take at all. An escalate-gated enforcement op still encodes\n  the concept in the actor's vocabulary and creates a one-step path\n  (a human clicking \"approve\") to real enforcement action being\n  attributed to a documentation robot. This ADR's actor has no such\n  op anywhere, gated or not.\n- A single HARD check keyed only on `:op` membership, without the\n  content-based scope-exclusion layer: rejected. An op-only check is\n  necessary but not sufficient — it does nothing to stop a\n  *legitimately-allowlisted* op (e.g. `:log-inspection-record`) whose\n  free-text `:rationale` nonetheless asserts that a search was\n  authorized or an entry was denied \"for documentation purposes.\" The\n  two-layer design closes that gap.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"search\" \"seizure\"\n  \"detention\" \"entry\"]`): rejected outright per the fleet-known\n  self-trip bug class described above — this actor's own domain\n  necessarily discusses search/seizure/detention/entry concepts to do\n  its documentation job honestly (its own default rationale text says\n  it is NOT proposing any of them), so a bare-noun list would have\n  self-blocked the actor's own legitimate proposals. Verified via two\n  dedicated regression tests, both green on first run.\n- A per-op numeric ceiling on `:log-inspection-record` (mirroring\n  3313's transaction-amount ceiling exactly): rejected as not\n  domain-appropriate — inspection-record logging is not a monetary\n  posting, so its HARD check is presence-of-source-document, not an\n  amount ceiling. The monetary-ceiling shape was instead reused, more\n  narrowly, for `:coordinate-supply-order`'s cost-threshold escalation\n  (registered per facility via `:max-supply-order-cost`, escalate not\n  hard-block, since procurement above budget is routine administrative\n  work needing sign-off, not a categorical prohibition).\n\n## References\n\n- `cloud-itonami-isco-3313/` (module-shape mirror — verified working\n  reference for the governed-actor pattern; its own registry\n  `:maturity` is `:implemented`)\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` `resources/kotoba/occupation/registry.edn`\n  `\"3351\"` entry\n- ADR-2607121000 (global ISIC/ISCO reverse-toposort rollout plan)\n- ADR-2607012000 (`cloud-itonami-isco-occupation-blueprints`)\n", :adr/date "2026-07-17", :adr/title "cloud-itonami-isco-3351 -- Customs and Border Inspectors Documentation Support (no search/seizure/detention/entry-denial authority)", :adr/source-format "edn", :adr/references [2607121000 2607012000], :adr/scope [:kotoba-lang/occupation :cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3351], :actor/id :cloud-itonami-isco-3351, :actor/name "Customs and Border Inspection Documentation & Logistics-Coordination Support", :actor/isco-08 "3351", :actor/domain :public-safety/customs-border-inspection-documentation, :actor/robotics? true, :actor/coordination? true, :actor/wave 2, :actor/proposal-ops [:log-inspection-record :schedule-lane-operation :flag-inspection-concern :coordinate-supply-order], :actor/always-escalate-ops [:flag-inspection-concern], :actor/auto-commit-ops [:log-inspection-record :schedule-lane-operation :coordinate-supply-order], :actor/hard-checks [:inspector-provenance :effect-propose :closed-op-allowlist :scope-excluded-op :scope-excluded-language :facility-basis :source-document-attached], :actor/cost-threshold-escalate-ops "{:coordinate-supply-order :facility-registered-max-supply-order-cost}", :actor/scope-excluded-terms ["authorize the search" "authorized the search" "authorizes the search" "authorize a search" "authorized a search" "authorizes a search" "order the seizure" "ordered the seizure" "orders the seizure" "order a seizure" "ordered a seizure" "orders a seizure" "order the detention" "ordered the detention" "orders the detention" "order a detention" "ordered a detention" "orders a detention" "deny entry" "denied entry" "denies entry" "denying entry" "issue the citation" "issued the citation" "issues the citation" "issue a citation" "issued a citation" "issues a citation" "issue the penalty" "issued the penalty" "issues the penalty" "issue a penalty" "issued a penalty" "issues a penalty"], :actor/scope-excluded-ops [:authorize-search :order-seizure :order-detention :deny-entry :issue-citation :issue-penalty], :registry/maturity :implemented, :registry/repo "https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3351", :registry/business-id "cloud-itonami-isco-3351", :registry/required-technologies [:robotics :identity :audit-ledger], :registry/operating-states [:intake :propose :approve :execute :audit], :operation/nodes [:intake :advise :govern :decide :request-approval :commit :hold], :operation/entry-point :intake, :test/counts "{:total 22, :assertions 70, :failures 0, :errors 0}", :test/coverage [:governor-hard-checks :advisor-shapes :integration :store-contract :scope-exclusion-self-trip-regression :closed-op-allowlist :facility-provenance :source-document-basis], :db/id -1}]